Stefan's law is \(E=\sigma\left(T^4\right)\)
\(\begin{aligned}
& \Rightarrow \sigma=\frac{E}{T^4} \\
& \text {where, } E=\frac{\text { Energy }}{\text { Area } \times \text { Time }}=\frac{\text { Watt }}{m^2} \\
& \sigma=\frac{\text { Watt }-m^2}{k^4}=\text { Watt }-m^{-2} k^{-4}
\end{aligned}\)

If $\mathrm{r}$ is the radius of the star and $T$ its temperature, then the energy emitted by the star per second through radiation in accordance with Stefan's law will be given by $A \sigma \mathrm{T}^4=4 \pi \mathrm{r}^2 \sigma \mathrm{T}^4$
In reaching a distance $R$ this energy will spread over a sphere of radius $R$; so the intensity of radiation will be given by

